### Code Implementation Guidelines

Follow these rules when you write code:

- Use early returns whenever possible to make the code more readable.
- Write code in a functional style: prefer pure functions, immutable data transformations (map/filter/reduce over mutations), and declarative patterns. Avoid class components, imperative loops where a declarative alternative exists, and direct state mutations.
- Keep imports clean and organized: combine multiple imports from the same module into a single import statement, remove unused imports after refactoring, and group imports in order: 1) React, 2) third-party libraries, 3) `@/` aliased project imports, 4) relative imports. Use barrel exports (`index.ts`) for public API of each module.
- Use descriptive variable and function/const names. Also, event functions should be named with a "handle" prefix, like "handleClick" for onClick and "handleKeyDown" for onKeyDown.
- Declare components as `export const ComponentName: React.FC<Props> = ({ ... }) => { ... }`. Use arrow functions assigned to `const` with explicit types for all functions and handlers.
- Avoid `any` and type assertions (`as`). Use proper generics, discriminated unions, or `unknown` with type guards.
- Handle errors explicitly: use try/catch in async operations, handle RTK Query error states in UI, use Error Boundaries for critical sections.
- Only add comments to explain non-obvious intent or business rules. Do not narrate what the code does.

### Accessibility (WCAG 2.2 AA)

Accessibility is a strict requirement. Code is validated with Lighthouse, NVDA, VoiceOver, and automated a11y testing. All new code must comply with WCAG 2.2 Level AA.

Semantic HTML & Structure:

- Use semantic HTML tags: `<button>`, `<a>`, `<nav>`, `<main>`, `<footer>`, `<section>`, `<article>` instead of generic `<div>` or `<span>` with click handlers.
- Maintain proper heading hierarchy (`h1` → `h2` → `h3`), never skip levels.
- Use landmarks (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<aside>`, `<footer>`) so screen readers can navigate by region.
- Provide a "Skip to main content" link or use ARIA landmarks to bypass repeated navigation blocks.
- Every page must have a descriptive `<title>`. Set correct `lang` attribute on `<html>`.
- All UI components must expose programmatic name, role, and state via native HTML or ARIA attributes.

Keyboard:

- All interactive elements must be reachable and operable via keyboard alone.
- Maintain a logical tab order. Use `tabIndex={0}` for custom interactive elements, never positive `tabIndex` values.
- Provide visible focus indicators on all focusable elements — never remove `:focus-visible` outlines without a styled replacement.
- No keyboard traps — users must be able to navigate away from any component using standard keys (Tab, Escape).
- When focus moves (e.g. modal open/close), manage focus programmatically to the appropriate element.
- Focused elements must never be obscured by overlays, sticky headers, or off-screen positioning.

Forms:

- Every input must have an explicit programmatic label (`<label htmlFor>` or `aria-label`).
- Mark required fields clearly (visual indicator + `aria-required="true"`).
- Group related fields with `<fieldset>` and `<legend>`.
- Use `autoComplete` attributes on user data fields (name, email, address, etc.).
- Place instructions and error messages near the relevant input. Announce errors via `aria-live` or `aria-describedby`.
- Avoid redundant entry — do not require users to enter the same information more than once in a flow.

Images & Media:

- Every `<img>` must have a meaningful `alt` attribute. Decorative images use `alt=""`.
- Icons and non-text UI elements must have `aria-label` or visually hidden text.
- Video content must include captions (`<track kind="captions">`). Audio-only content must have transcripts.

Color & Contrast:

- Text must meet a 4.5:1 contrast ratio (3:1 for large text ≥18px bold or ≥24px regular).
- Non-text UI elements (icons, borders, focus indicators) must meet 3:1 contrast ratio.
- Never use color alone to convey meaning (e.g. error states must also have icons or text labels).

Interactive Elements & Touch:

- Clickable/tappable targets must be at least 24×24px.
- All actions must be achievable with a single pointer (click or tap). Provide alternatives for complex gestures (drag, multi-finger).
- Content that appears on hover/focus (tooltips, popovers) must be dismissible (Escape), hoverable without disappearing, and accessible via keyboard.

Status & Notifications:

- Use ARIA live regions (`aria-live="polite"` or `"assertive"`) for dynamic status messages (form errors, confirmations, loading states).
- Do not rely solely on visual changes — screen readers must be informed of state transitions.

Responsive & Visual:

- Support both portrait and landscape orientations.
- All content must remain functional and readable at 200% browser zoom with no horizontal scrolling.
- Ensure no loss of content or functionality if users apply custom stylesheets or override colors/fonts.
- No flashing content or rapid animations that could cause seizures (max 3 flashes per second).

Bug severity classification:

- Critical: blocks access entirely (missing alt text, keyboard traps, no focus management).
- High: major barriers (poor contrast, broken heading structure, missing form labels).
- Medium: impacts experience but not access (missing skip links, suboptimal tab order).
- Low: minor or intermittent issues.

### Architecture: Feature-Sliced Design (FSD)

The project follows Feature-Sliced Design with strict layer dependency rules.
Global layers (top to bottom, upper depends on lower, never reverse):
app → src/views → src/widgets → src/features → src/entities → src/shared
Rules:

- A lower layer MUST NOT import from an upper layer (e.g. `shared` cannot import from `features`, `entities` cannot import from `widgets`)
- Each layer can only depend on layers below it
- Cross-imports within the same layer are prohibited (e.g. one feature cannot import from another feature)
  Dependency flow:
  src/
  ├── app/ ← nextjs root folder
  ├── src/views - fsd widgets export inside app nestjs folder eq pages in classic fsd
  ├── src/widgets ← fsd widgets
  ├── src/features/ ← fsd features
  ├── src/entities/ ← fsd entities
  └── src/shared/ ← fsd shared (ui, api, hooks, types, lib)

### File Naming and Component Structure

- Atomic components; target ~100 lines per file. Exceptions: config files, generated files, barrel `index.ts`
- Components must be "dumb" — responsible only for rendering. All business logic, data preparation, and event handlers live in the `.conf.ts` hook
- Not every file is required — create only what is needed. A simple component/view may be a single `.tsx`

File naming convention (closed set — do NOT invent a new suffix, e.g. `.styled.ts`, `.helpers.ts`, `.model.ts`, without asking the user first):
ComponentName.tsx — UI only: renders JSX, no logic, no data transformations
ComponentName.conf.ts — Business layer: custom hook `useComponentNameConfig` that returns all props, handlers, and computed values for the component. Over ~100 lines → split into a `hooks/` subfolder (`use<Purpose>.ts` per file) instead of one large `.conf.ts`
ComponentName.types.ts — Type definitions. Prefer `type` over `interface` (interfaces are extendable and can be redeclared, use `interface` only when extension is intentionally needed). If types are few, they can live in `.conf.ts` or next to `.tsx` if no `.conf.ts` exists
ComponentName.constants.ts — Hardcoded constants only (NOT i18n strings — use next-intl `useTranslations()`/`getTranslations()` reading `i18n/messages/{locale}/*.json`)
ComponentName.utils.ts — Pure utility functions: data transformations, formatting, mapping. No hooks, no side effects. Over ~100 lines → split into a `lib/` subfolder
ComponentName.schema.ts — Zod schemas for validation, bridged via `@hookform/resolvers` `zodResolver`. Never `.validation.ts`, never Yup/Formik
index.ts — Barrel export for public API of the module
Standalone hooks (reusable logic shared across components):
use\*.ts — Custom hooks in `hooks/` folders. One hook per file, named `use<Purpose>.ts`

Styling: Tailwind classNames + `cva()` (class-variance-authority) + the `cn()` utility (`src/shared/lib/styles/cn.ts`). No MUI, no `styled()` API, no separate styling file — styles live directly on JSX in `.tsx` via `cn()`/`cva()`.

Example of a typical component set:
ItemCard.tsx:
import { cn } from '@/src/shared/lib/styles/cn'
import { useItemCardConfig } from './ItemCard.conf'
export const ItemCard: React.FC<ItemCardProps> = (props) => {
const { title, isActive, handleClick, ... } = useItemCardConfig(props)
return <div className={cn('rounded-md p-4', isActive && 'bg-accent')} onClick={handleClick}>...</div>
}
ItemCard.conf.ts:
export const useItemCardConfig = ({ item, onToggle }: ItemCardProps) => {
// all business logic, data mapping, handlers
return { title, isActive, handleClick, handleKeyDown, ... }
}
ItemCard.constants.ts:
export const MAX_TITLE_LENGTH = 80

- When component grows: extract sub-components into a `ui/` subfolder with the same file conventions

### Testing

- Target 80% code coverage. When modifying untested code, add tests alongside the changes
- Testing priority (highest to lowest):
  1. Business logic: utility functions, helpers, data transformations
  2. Custom hooks: state transitions, side effects, edge cases (use `renderHook` + `act`)
  3. Component behavior: user interactions, conditional rendering, integration with hooks
  4. Pure rendering: only when needed to reach coverage target
- Use Jest + React Testing Library. Prefer `userEvent` over `fireEvent` for user interactions — it simulates real browser behavior (focus, pointer, keyboard events)
- Test file placement: `__tests__/` folder next to the module, or `*.test.ts(x)` colocated with the source file
- Mock data files: place in `__tests__/mocks/` folder as `*.mock.ts`. Name should reflect the data domain: `<entity/feature>.mock.ts` (e.g. `items.mock.ts`). Use factory functions (`createMockItem(overrides)`) over static objects — this keeps tests isolated and avoids shared mutable state
- Write focused, isolated tests: one behavior per `it()` block, descriptive test names that explain the expected outcome, minimal setup with no shared mutable state between tests
- Mock external dependencies (API calls, timers, third-party modules) — never test implementation details, test observable behavior
- Use `@/` path alias in test imports. Use `jest.fn()` for callback props, `jest.spyOn` for method mocking with cleanup

### Commit Guidelines (Conventional Commits)

The project uses `@commitlint/config-conventional`. All commit messages must follow this format:
`<type>: <description>`

Allowed types: `feat`, `fix`, `refactor`, `perf`, `style`, `test`, `docs`, `build`, `ci`, `chore`, `revert`.
Scope is optional and should reflect the affected area, e.g. `feat(users): add search filter`.

Rules:

- Use the imperative mood in the subject line: "add feature" not "added feature".
- Do not end the subject line with a period.
- Keep the subject line concise (72 characters or less).
- Use the body to explain what and why, not how.
- Reference: https://www.conventionalcommits.org
